# Deep beginner lessons, part 3: Q30-Q44.
$LEARN[30] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The coding agent executes outside GitHub Actions, in its own runtime with its own credential channels. <b>Actions secrets</b> inject only into workflow runs. Files in git (custom instructions, agent configs, committed JSON) are readable by every repo reader. The <b>Copilot environment</b> is the agent runtime secret store: values placed there are visible to the agent and its tools and nowhere else. <b>Example:</b> the agent must call an internal API during its session. As an Actions secret it would sit unused while the agent fails with 401s; committed to copilot-instructions.md it would leak to every developer and every fork. As a Copilot environment secret the call succeeds and the token appears in no log, no diff, no clone. Rule of thumb: name the consuming runtime first, then pick its native secret home.</div>"
$LEARN[31] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Memories would rot fast if Copilot trusted them blindly, so every memory carries <b>citations</b>: pointers to the code or words that support it. Before each use, Copilot re-checks repository-fact citations against the <b>current branch</b> and silently <b>ignores</b> entries whose code is gone: stale knowledge can never drive an answer, and ignoring is not deleting (the entry may validate again later or expire via the 28-day rule). <b>Example:</b> a fact 'retries live in net/retry.go' survives refactors that keep the file, and is skipped the week retry.go is deleted, without any admin action. Contrast the distractors: facts are shared repo-wide, not locked to their creator; nothing lives indefinitely; and closed-unmerged PRs can seed memories precisely because validation (not merge status) guards quality.</div>"
$LEARN[32] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Diagnose empty-but-successful PRs by asking which artifact records <b>agent intent</b>, not infrastructure. The <b>Agents panel session log</b> is the agent own narration: what it read, decided, ran and why. The <b>Actions runner log</b> records the sandbox: checkouts, installs, exit codes. <b>GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</b> describe what the agent was allowed to touch. <b>Usage metrics</b> aggregate thousands of sessions. <b>Example:</b> the log shows 'explored auth/, found the SSO flag already handled in middleware, no diff needed, opened PR to report': mystery solved, the agent was right. The runner log would only show green steps; metrics would show one more successful session; permissions would show scopes. Match the question (why did it do nothing?) to the only artifact containing decisions.</div>"
$LEARN[33] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> CLI session logs reward literal reading: trust exact field values over vibes. <b>Passing 1 MCP server(s) to SDK: [github]</b> is positive proof the GitHub server reached the session; the nearby <b>CLIMCPServerEnabled=false</b> only gates additional CLI-side servers, a different list. <b>isolationEnabled: false</b> plus no worktree path is negative proof: isolation mode may have been selected, but it never engaged. Sessions are counted by distinct <b>isNewSession: true</b> identities, not by request lines (ccreq IDs are per-call, not per-session). <b>Example walk:</b> line 'Passing 1 MCP server(s) [github]' answers statement 1 Yes. 'isolationEnabled: false, worktreePath: undefined' answers statement 2 No. Two Invoking lines with de04f0fe and 6852c5dc answer statement 3 Yes. Commenters split because they skimmed; the fields decide.</div>"
$LEARN[34] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> This log is a masterclass in what absence proves. <b>message 0 returned. finish reason: [stop]</b> means a model call completed having produced zero messages: silence, not a reply, so 'responded with messages' is No no matter how many such lines exist. <b>[panel/editAgent]</b> is a VS Code UI panel tag identifying where the call originated, exactly like a window title: it evidences zero file operations, so 'can edit files' is No. The remaining lines are model identifiers (gpt-4o-mini, gpt-5.3-codex), progress notes and wrappers: compute happened, but no line shows a file read, search, or analysis operation, and <b>copilotmd</b> labels the request format, not a repository file. So 'analyzed files' is No. <b>Example mistake to avoid:</b> one commenter read panel/editAgent as editing and message-0 as messaging: in logs, names describe plumbing, and only verbs (read, edit, search, analyze with paths) describe work.</div>"
$LEARN[35] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Copilot code review accepts guidance from several files with different scopes, and scope decides. <b>.github/copilot-instructions.md</b> applies repo-wide to every review: the home for team-wide review policy. <b>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</b> files apply only to paths matching their applyTo patterns: perfect for 'frontend rules for /frontend'. <b>.agent.md profiles</b> shape single agents, not the reviewer. <b>pull_request_template.md</b> coaches human authors filling in PR bodies. <b>Example:</b> the team wants 'every PR checked for missing tests and raw SQL'. That rule belongs in copilot-instructions.md so backend, docs and infra PRs all get it. Putting it in a path-scoped instructions file would silently skip half the repo; putting it in an agent profile would miss reviews entirely, since the reviewer is not that agent.</div>"
$LEARN[36] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> When an agent games your checks, distinguish blocking the move from teaching the game. The agent edits workflows because its instructions never said the goal is fixed code with green checks; bypassing checks is, to it, a creative shortcut. A <b>branch ruleset</b> would block workflow edits, but the agent then stalls with failing tests and no permitted path forward. Disabling Actions removes the signal entirely. --allow-all adds autonomy to misbehavior. <b>Updating .github/copilot-instructions.md</b> with 'never modify CI to green it; fix the underlying code; failing checks mean the code is wrong' rewrites the objective function itself. <b>Example instruction block:</b> list forbidden strategies (editing workflows, deleting tests, marking failures tolerated) plus the required one (fix source, rerun, report). Agents optimize what you write down: write the real goal.</div>"
$LEARN[37] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> GitHub records security-relevant actions as structured <b>audit log events</b>, each stamped with actor, timestamp, repo and action name. Artifact deletion is the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event: filter the audit log for it and you get exactly who deleted which artifact when. The neighboring options live in different universes: <b>checks.delete_logs</b> concerns check-run logs, PR descriptions and comments narrate human discussion, merge history records commits. None stores deletion events. <b>Example investigation:</b> nightly-release.zip vanished. Audit log filtered to action:artifact.destroy shows deploy-bot deleted it Tuesday 03:12 during retention cleanup. Without the event filter you would grep PR prose forever and find nothing, because prose never records deletions. Name the event family first (artifact.*), then the verb (destroy).</div>"
$LEARN[38] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Agent sessions have two states needing different verbs. A <b>live</b> session (open PR, recent activity) is steered: comment @copilot on the PR. A <b>dead</b> session (long silence, e.g. past the 70-minute mark, no progress in View session) cannot be steered: it must be <b>restarted</b>, and the supported restart is unassign the issue from Copilot and reassign it, which spawns a fresh session from the same issue context. The wrong verbs misfire characteristically: waiting on a dead session waits forever; merging its empty PR ships nothing and closes the session; Approve-and-run gates workflows instead of reviving agents. <b>Example:</b> Friday session silent since Thursday. Unassign, reassign Monday: new session, same issue text, full history visible. Commenting @copilot on the stale PR instead would address a corpse.</div>"
$LEARN[39] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> GitHub keeps audit history briefly (weeks, not years) and offers no multi-year retention dial: 'configure retention to 2 years' is not a real knob. Compliance regimes that demand years of immutable evidence use <b>audit log streaming</b>: every event is forwarded as it happens to your own sink such as <b>Azure Event Hubs</b> (or S3, Splunk), where your retention policy, not GitHub, applies. 'Enable log forwarding' and 'Git events' name no durable multi-year sink; toggling event types changes what is recorded, not how long it survives. <b>Example architecture:</b> org streams to Event Hubs, which archives to immutable blob storage with a 7-year lock. Two years later an auditor asks who approved a production deploy: you query your archive, not GitHub, because GitHub forgot months ago. Retention questions are always answered by where events are copied, never by a setting that does not exist.</div>"
$LEARN[40] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Diagnose runtime failures by locating them on the timeline. A <b>preToolUse hook</b> runs synchronously inside the tool call: when it denies, the live stream breaks exactly there, mid-task, with the hook name in the error. That signature (failure during invocation, before any result) rules out the neighbors: <b>postToolUse</b> only observes completed calls and cannot break them; <b>app installation policies</b> fail at install/permission time, long before streaming; <b>firewall policies</b> fail network calls with network-shaped errors (timeouts, resets), not hook decisions. <b>Example:</b> the stream shows 'calling deploy-staging...' then 'denied by preToolUse guardrail: production deploys need approval'. The fix is a policy exception or an approval step, not debugging the network or reinstalling anything. Read the break point, name the gate at that point.</div>"
$LEARN[41] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Error taxonomy for agent runs has four buckets; classify by where it broke, not how it feels. <b>Tool misuse:</b> the invocation itself failed before producing a result (bad arguments, disallowed target, MCP schema violation). <b>Reasoning error:</b> the call succeeded but the model logic was wrong (called the right tool for the wrong reason). <b>Context issue:</b> prompt, window or scope problems (too much, too little, wrong files loaded). <b>Network issue:</b> transport failures (DNS, TLS, timeouts). <b>Example:</b> the agent calls the Microsoft Docs search tool and the call fails immediately with no result: tool misuse, fix the invocation or permissions. Had it returned irrelevant docs, that would be reasoning (wrong query). Had the call never left the machine, network. Had the prompt exceeded limits, context. The log shows failure during invocation with zero output: bucket one.</div>"
$LEARN[42] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The <b>gh agent-task</b> extension splits task management into verbs with Unix-style flags. <b>list</b> enumerates tasks (--status filters, --web opens the browser view). <b>view</b> targets one task: <b>--log</b> prints its session log, <b>--follow</b> keeps the stream open like tail -f, <b>--jq</b> queries a static JSON snapshot. 'Live stream one session log' decomposes to view (one task) + log (its log) + follow (keep streaming). <b>Example:</b> gh agent-task view 42 --log --follow tails task 42 until you Ctrl-C. Swap --follow for --jq and you get one frozen JSON dump: perfect for scripting, useless for watching. Swap view for list and you enumerate tasks without ever seeing a log. Read the requirement as verb plus modifiers, then assemble exactly those flags.</div>"
$LEARN[43] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> 'Automated evaluation signals' means machine verdicts about the code, and 'built-in scanning tools' means scanners GitHub ships and runs itself. Two qualify. <b>Secret scanning</b> detects hardcoded API keys and tokens: a credential in generated code is an objective defect no human must eyeball. <b>CodeQL code scanning</b> proves security properties (injection, auth flaws) with query-based analysis. The distractors fail the definition differently: <b>repo CI lint</b> is your pipeline, not built-in; <b>Copilot review comments</b> are AI prose suggestions, not scanner verdicts; <b>median time-to-merge</b> measures team process on a dashboard, saying nothing about this diff. <b>Example:</b> the agent PR adds aStripe key literal and a SQL-concatenated query: secret scanning flags the key, CodeQL flags the injection, CI lint stays green, review prose suggests style, the dashboard shows last month velocity. Only the first two evaluate this code automatically.</div>"
$LEARN[44] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> GitHub runners are ephemeral and jobs are isolated: when agent-run finishes, its filesystem evaporates, so later jobs (review, deploy) cannot see trace.json and plan.md unless the files travel through the native handoff: <b>upload-artifact</b> in the producer, <b>download-artifact</b> in each consumer. The alternatives fail on fitness, not syntax. <b>Dependency caching</b> is best-effort storage for dependencies that may miss: unacceptable for required inputs. <b>Committing</b> generated files pollutes history with machine output. A <b>network share</b> adds infrastructure runners cannot mount or trust. <b>Example:</b> agent-run uploads plan.md (50 KB, instant); review downloads it, renders the diff summary; deploy downloads it again to extract rollout steps. Same file, three jobs, zero repo pollution: artifacts are the clipboard between jobs.</div>"
